.TH KARIDNS 8 "2026-11-14" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns \- Authoritative DNS Server Daemon for FreeBSD
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karidns [\-v | \-\-version | \-V] [\-f] [\-d debug_level] [\-p port | \-p pid_file] [\-P pid_file] [\-c config_file | config_file]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkaridns\fR is an authoritative DNS server designed for FreeBSD. It uses a
privilege-separated multi-process architecture with FreeBSD \fBCapsicum\fR
sandboxing, atomic RCU-based configuration/zone management, and pre-allocated
memory arenas.
.SS Architectural Structure
.IP 1. 4
\fBPrivilege Separation & Capsicum Sandboxing\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBManager (Supervisor) Process\fR: The process started by the administrator. It
parses the configuration, performs the startup checks, forks the other processes
and supervises them: if any child exits, all children are stopped and the
manager exits. The exceptions are \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR (the database loader,
see \fBdatabase { ... }\fR below) and, since 0.6.2, the plugins of \fBtype
program\fR zones (PROGRAM ZONE PLUGINS): they are restarted after 1 s, 2 s, 4
s ... 60 s while the Backend keeps answering. \fBSIGHUP\fR is forwarded to the
backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFrontend Router Processes\fR: Bind the privileged network sockets (UDP/TCP
port 53) and dispatch network traffic to the backend workers. One router is
started on hosts with up to 3 CPU cores, two on larger hosts.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBackend Process\fR: Operates in FreeBSD Capsicum capability mode
(\fBcap_enter(2)\fR). DNS packet parsing and response generation are performed
by the worker threads (one or two on hosts with up to 3 cores, otherwise the
number of cores minus two) without direct filesystem access or socket creation
permissions. Configuration and zone files are accessed via pre-opened directory
descriptors (\fBopenat(2)\fR / \fBrenameat(2)\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCPUs\fR: the routers and the worker threads are not pinned to CPUs (since
0.6.1), so the scheduler can move a thread that another busy thread displaces;
pinned workers used to keep the queries already handed to them for 0.5 to 2 s
when something else on the host used a whole CPU. To keep other work
(\fBkaridns\-signer\fR, PostgreSQL) off the server's CPUs, give that work a CPU
set of its own with \fBcpuset(1)\fR instead. When you measure with \fBdnsperf\fR
on the same host, run it with \fBnice \-n 20\fR or from another host: dnsperf
2.14 busy-loops at the end of a run, and the time its threads take from the
server shows as answers of 0.5 to 2 s.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBConnect Broker\fR: A small unprivileged helper that opens the outbound
connections the sandboxed backend cannot create itself (zone transfers of
secondary zones from their primary, forward-zone queries, and reconnecting to
the dnstap collector). It starts a non-blocking \fBconnect(2)\fR and hands the
socket back at once; the backend waits for the connection to complete within its
own time limit, so one slow server does not hold up other outbound
connections. It only connects to UNIX sockets for the \fBdnstap\fR socket
configured at startup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStartup order\fR: user and group are looked up once, before any thread is
started, and the same identity is used by all processes and for log and
control-socket ownership. Control commands (\fBkarictl\fR), \fBSIGHUP\fR and the
control thread's timers are processed only after the backend has dropped
privileges and entered capability mode.
.IP \(bu 2
If the kernel has no \fBCAPABILITY_MODE\fR (\fBcap_enter(2)\fR fails with
\fBENOSYS\fR), the backend logs \fBCapsicum is not available ... running WITHOUT
the capability\-mode sandbox\fR at \fBLOG_CRIT\fR and keeps running. Any other
\fBcap_enter(2)\fR failure aborts startup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBShutdown\fR: on \fBSIGTERM\fR/\fBSIGINT\fR, \fBkarictl stop\fR, or when the
manager process goes away, the backend sends the dnstap frames that are still
queued, ends the Frame Streams session (STOP / FINISH) and exits. A second
signal exits at once.
.RE
.IP 2. 4
\fBRead-Copy-Update (RCU) Architecture\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Zone data and configuration pointers are swapped atomically using C11 atomic
operations (\fBmemory_order_acquire\fR / \fBmemory_order_release\fR), allowing
worker threads to serve queries concurrently during zone reloads without
locking.
.RE
.IP 3. 4
\fBMemory Arena Allocator (\fBzone_arena_t\fB)\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
For ordinary read-only queries (e.g. standard \fBQUERY\fR lookups) over UDP and
TCP, dynamic memory allocations (\fBmalloc\fR/\fBfree\fR) are not used; stack
buffers and bump-allocated memory arenas (\fBzone_arena_t\fR) are used for
request handling. TCP connection state comes from a pool of 1000 connection
contexts allocated when the backend starts (memory pages are only used once a
context has been used), and each worker thread builds its TCP responses in a
buffer allocated at startup.
.IP \(bu 2
Exceptions:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic Update (\fBRFC 2136\fR, OPCODE=5): applying an update thaws the zone's
published image into a builder (\fBthaw_zone_arena\fR, using \fBrealloc\fR),
applies the update, freezes a new image and publishes it as a new version (the
old version is freed after the RCU grace period), and computes an IXFR diff
(\fBcompute_ixfr_diff\fR, using \fBmalloc\fR) so that secondaries can be
notified incrementally. This path is synchronous with the query but is
inherently a write path, not the hot read path.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype forward\fR and \fBtype program\fR zones: the query is copied to the heap
and handed to a separate pool of I/O threads through a queue protected by a
mutex, so that waiting for the forwarder or the program does not block a worker
thread.
.IP \(bu 2
Outgoing zone transfers (AXFR/IXFR): each transfer runs in its own thread, which
receives its parameters in a heap block.
.RE
.RE
.IP 4. 4
\fBKqueue Event Loop\fR:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Network I/O events for TCP connections and UDP sockets are managed using FreeBSD
\fBkqueue(2)\fR.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS & ARGUMENTS
.TP 8
\fB<config_file>\fR, \fB\-c config_file\fR
Path to the configuration file (e.g.,
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf\fR). This argument is required; there is
no built-in default path. Any argument that is not one of the options below is
taken as the configuration file.
.TP 8
\fB\-f\fR
Run in the foreground instead of daemonizing into the background. In foreground
mode, no PID file is created unless one is set explicitly (\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-p
<path>\fR or \fBpid\-file\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-d debug_level\fR
The debug level in force from the start, 0\[u2013]99 (BIND's \fBnamed \-d\fR;
default 0). A value that is not a number from 0 to 99, or \fB\-d\fR without a
value, is a usage error (exit status 2). The level decides what the channels
with \fBseverity dynamic\fR and the built-in \fBdefault_debug\fR (the file
\fBkaridns.run\fR) write; \fBkarictl trace\fR and \fBnotrace\fR change it while
the server runs and \fBkarictl status\fR shows it. See \fBSeverities and the
debug level\fR under \fBlogging { ... }\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-p port\fR, \fB\-p pid_file\fR
The meaning depends on the value. If the whole value is a decimal number, it is
the \fBlisten port\fR (1\[u2013]65535; other numbers are an error) and overrides
\fBoptions { port ...; }\fR (also on reload); for example \fB\-p 10053\fR. Any
other value is taken as the path of the PID lock file, like \fB\-P\fR, with a
warning that recommends \fB\-P\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-P pid_file\fR
Path to the PID lock file (overrides \fBoptions { pid\-file "..."; }\fR;
default: \fB/var/run/karidns/karidns.pid\fR when daemonized). Specify \fBnone\fR
to disable PID locking.
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR, \fB\-V\fR
Print the version information and exit.
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR \fBUser, Group, and Sandboxing Controls:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Process privileges (\fBuser\fR and \fBgroup\fR) are configured exclusively via
the \fBoptions { user "..."; group "..."; }\fR directive in the configuration
file rather than command-line arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
Traditional \fBchroot\fR is not implemented; filesystem and system call
sandboxing is enforced via FreeBSD native \fBCapsicum\fR (\fBcap_enter(2)\fR
capability mode) in the backend worker process.
.RE
.SS Running as a non-root user
\fBkaridns\fR does not have to be started as root. There are two supported ways
to run it:
.PP
.TS
lb lb lbw(32)
l l lw(32).
\&Started as	\&\fBoptions { user ...; }\fR	\&Behavior
_
\&root	\&set	T{
Binds sockets and opens files as root, then drops to \fBuser\fR/\fBgroup\fR
(recommended for port 53).
T}
\&root	\&not set	T{
Refused: running as root without a privilege drop is not permitted (also with
only \fBgroup\fR set, which would keep uid 0).
T}
\&non-root user \fIU\fR	\&not set	\&Runs as \fIU\fR.
\&non-root user \fIU\fR	\&\fBuser "U";\fR (itself)	T{
Runs as \fIU\fR; the privilege drop is skipped.
T}
\&non-root user \fIU\fR	\&another user	T{
Refused: a non-root process cannot switch users.
T}
.TE
.PP
When started as a non-root user, \fBgroup\fR (if set) must be that user's
current primary group, and \fBprogram\-user\fR of \fBtype program\fR zones
(which defaults to \fBoptions { user }\fR) must be that user as well.
.PP
Everything the process needs must then be usable by that user. \fBkaridns
refuses to start\fR (non-zero exit status and an \fB[ERROR]\fR message on stderr
and syslog) instead of running half-broken when:
.IP \(bu 2
the listen port cannot be bound over UDP \fBor\fR TCP \[u2014] ports below 1024
(such as 53) require root, so use e.g. \fBport 10053;\fR or \fB\-p 10053\fR; a
port already in use by another process is refused too;
.IP \(bu 2
a \fBlogging { channel { file "..."; }; }\fR file cannot be opened (e.g. under
\fB/var/log\fR); choose a directory the user can write to;
.IP \(bu 2
the PID file cannot be created: when daemonized, the default is
\fB/var/run/karidns/karidns.pid\fR, so pass \fB\-P <path>\fR / \fBpid\-file
"<path>";\fR, or \fBpid\-file "none";\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontrol\-channel\fR is enabled and its UNIX socket cannot be bound: the
default is \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR, so set \fBcontrol\-channel {
socket "<path>"; ... };\fR to a path in a directory the user can write to (and
point \fBkarictl\fR at the same path);
.IP \(bu 2
another instance is running: its PID file is locked, or a server answers on the
control socket (\fBAnother KariDNS instance is already running\fR). The running
instance's PID file and control socket are left as they are.
.PP
These checks run \fBbefore\fR the process daemonizes, so in daemon mode the
failure is reported to the invoking shell/rc script as well. The same
fail-closed rules apply when started as root (a log file that cannot be opened,
an unbindable port or control socket all abort startup). On \fBkarictl
reconfig\fR / \fBSIGHUP\fR, a configuration whose log files cannot be opened is
rejected and the running configuration is kept.
.PP
Example (\fB/home/dns/karidns.conf\fR, started by user \fBdns\fR with \fBkaridns
/home/dns/karidns.conf\fR):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options {
    port 10053;
    user "dns";                          // optional: the invoking user itself
    pid\-file "/home/dns/run/karidns.pid";
};
logging {
    channel queries_log { file "/home/dns/log/queries.log" versions 3 size 10M; };
    category queries { queries_log; };
};
control\-channel {
    socket "/home/dns/run/control.sock";
    algorithm "hmac\-sha256";
    secret "...";
};
.fi
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is described in karidns.conf(5).
.SH ROLLBACK (karictl rollback)
A reload with a mistake in the zone file, or a bad change in the database, is
served at once. \fBkarictl rollback <zone> [view] [local]\fR puts the zone back
to what it served before. It is for \fBprimary zones\fR (a zone file or a
database zone); a secondary gets its data from its primary, and catalog zones
and \fBprogram\fR zones are refused with an \fBERROR\fR.
.PP
\fBWhat is kept.\fR When a \fBzone-file reload\fR (\fBkarictl reload\fR,
\fBreload <zone>\fR, \fBreconfig\fR that reads the file, \fBSIGHUP\fR) or an
\fBinstall from the database\fR replaces the version a zone serves, the old
version is kept as the zone's \fIprevious version\fR for \fBrollback\-window\fR
(default \fB1h\fR, \fB0\fR = keep nothing; \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR or
\fBzone\fR). Only the version before the last reload is kept: a new reload
replaces it (the older one is freed), and a dynamic UPDATE or a zone transfer
does not touch it, so \fBrollback\fR goes back to before the reload and drops
the updates made since. A reload that brings exactly the content the zone has
already does not replace it. The previous version is not reachable by the query
path (no reader sees it), so queries cost what they cost without it; the control
thread's tick frees it when the window is over. Its bytes are the class
\fBprevious\fR of the zone memory totals (\fBkarictl status\fR, \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR, \fBkarictl zonestatus\fR for the one zone), and with a database
\fBmemory\-limit\fR previous versions are dropped before any zone is taken out
of memory (see "Memory limit"). Every path that frees a zone (removal in a
reconfig, a database delete, shutdown) frees its previous version too.
.PP
\fBSerial, IXFR, NOTIFY (RFC 1982, RFC 1995, RFC 1996).\fR The old content is
published with the SOA serial of the \fBcurrent\fR version plus one (RFC 1982
serial arithmetic; after 4294967295 the serial is 1, as for a dynamic UPDATE),
so a secondary sees an ordinary newer serial and never a serial that goes back
(RFC 1034 4.3.5: a lower serial is not accepted). The difference between the
version in service and the old content is recorded for IXFR and NOTIFY is sent,
as for a reload. \fBlocal\fR publishes the old version \fBunchanged, with its
old serial, on this server only\fR: the secondaries do not follow (a secondary
that has the newer serial keeps it and one that is behind transfers the whole
zone), no NOTIFY is sent and the IXFR history is dropped.
.PP
\fBSigned zones.\fR KariDNS serves pre-signed zones and cannot sign. A new
serial would leave the signature of the SOA that does not match, so the rollback
of a zone whose previous version holds RRSIG or DNSKEY records is refused
(\fBzone is signed; roll back in the DB and re\-sign\fR). \fBrollback <zone>
local\fR is allowed: the old version, with its old serial and its signatures, on
this server.
.PP
\fBAfter a rollback.\fR The previous version is used up: a second rollback is
refused until the zone is reloaded again (no redo). \fBkarictl zonestatus\fR
shows \fBrolled back (serial N, from M)\fR until the next publication (a reload,
an UPDATE, a database install).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone file.\fR The file still has the data that were rolled back. \fBreload
<zone>\fR and a full \fBreload\fR keep the rolled-back version while the file is
the one that was read (the same modification time and size) and say so; a
changed file is read as usual. \fBreload <zone> force\fR reads the file again
(and brings the bad data back), and so does a restart. The check looks at the
file the zone names, not at the files it \fB$INCLUDE\fRs: use \fBforce\fR after
a change in an included file. Fix the file and reload.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDatabase zone.\fR The server decides whether the loader's zone is new by the
source id, serial and digest it recorded when it installed the zone (the loader
compares its own with what the server sent in its inventory); a rollback leaves
that record alone. So \fBreload <zone>\fR answers \fBunchanged\fR, a full
\fBreload\fR and a restarted loader send nothing for the zone, and the
rolled-back version stays until the data in the database change; the next change
is installed as usual and ends the rolled-back state. \fBreload <zone> force\fR
sends the database's data again. While it is rolled back the zone is pinned in
memory (see "Memory limit"). \fBWith several primaries on one database\fR a
rollback on one server makes the servers differ from each other until the
database changes: the other primaries serve the new data. The durable fix is to
roll the change back in the database.
.IP \(bu 2
A restricted control key may be given the command: \fBallow\-commands {
rollback; };\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
The command is logged at NOTICE with the key name, like the others, and the
rollback itself is logged: \fB[Zone] Zone 'example.test.' rolled back: serial 7
(was 6)\fR.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options { rollback\-window 2h; };
zone "example.test" { type master; file "example.test.zone"; };
zone "scratch.test" { type master; file "scratch.test.zone"; rollback\-window 0; };   # keeps nothing
.fi
.RE
.SH CONTROL CHANNEL & MANAGEMENT
Runtime administration of \fBkaridns\fR is managed over a local UNIX domain
socket (default \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR, see \fBcontrol\-channel\fR)
authenticated via an HMAC challenge-response (\fBcontrol\-channel { algorithm
}\fR, default HMAC-SHA256) using the \fBkarictl(8)\fR utility. A key can be
restricted to some commands (\fBcontrol\-channel { keys { } }\fR, see above);
every command is logged with the name of the key.
.PP
\fBReloads and long readers (0.7).\fR A reload (\fBkarictl reload\fR,
\fBreconfig\fR, \fBSIGHUP\fR), a database batch and a dynamic UPDATE replace
zone versions and snapshots; the old ones are freed once no thread of the server
can still read them (the grace period of the epoch RCU). A thread that stays
long in a read section does not make any of them wait: a reload answers as soon
as the configuration and the zones are applied, and the memory the old versions
take stays allocated until that thread leaves, then it is freed by the control
thread's tick and the reclaim thread. During that time \fBkarictl status\fR
shows it (\fBretired zone snapshots waiting\fR, \fBretired zone versions
waiting\fR, rising and then falling back to 0) and after 60 s the server writes
the warning \fB[GC] the oldest retired zone snapshot has waited ...\fR naming
what holds it. Memory grows by the versions replaced meanwhile: a full reload
during such a reader keeps a second copy of every zone it re-read. For database
zones \fBmemory\-limit\fR is a target (up to about 1.5 times the value in normal
operation, see "Memory limit"): the retired versions such a reader holds
(\fBheld\fR in the \fBcache:\fR line of \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR) are not weighed
against it, so the cache manager neither takes zones out nor leaves a batch's
zones out for memory only the reader's leaving frees, and the memory in use
passes the limit by them until the reader leaves (measured: 3000 database zones
under \fBmemory\-limit 2m\fR, three batches during a 90 s reader: 1.6 times the
limit); then they are freed at once. The one thing that does wait is a reload
while a reader may still hold the configuration before the last one (the
configuration is kept in two buffers): an explicit reload (\fBkarictl reload\fR,
\fBreconfig\fR, \fBSIGHUP\fR) waits for such readers as long as they can last in
normal operation (the longest \fBprogram\-timeout\fR or \fBforward\-timeout\fR
of the configuration or of the one before, under which those readers began, plus
half a second, at least 1 s, at most 5 s), and if one is still there it answers
\fBERROR reload postponed\fR and the server applies the reload by itself, with
everything it was to do (for \fBkarictl reload\fR the request to the database
loader), within a second of that reader leaving. A reload command while one is
postponed does not wait again: it is merged into the postponed one (a full
reload, the request to the loader) and answers \fBERROR reload postponed\fR
after a short try (0.1 s) unless the readers have left.  The log says so once,
then at most once a minute while it waits, and once when the reload is
applied. Since 0.7 a zone transfer that waits for its client is not such a
reader (see "Outbound transfers").
.SH PROGRAM ZONE PLUGINS (TEST-ONLY FEATURE)
KariDNS supports dynamic external program-backed zones (\fBtype program;\fR)
exclusively for testing and anomaly fuzzing.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options {
    allow\-program\-zones yes; # Required to enable type program zones
};
\&
zone "anomaly.test." {
    type program;
    program "/usr/local/bin/mock_server.pl";
    program\-args { "\-\-verbose"; };
    program\-timeout 2000; # timeout in milliseconds (default: 2000)
    program\-max\-failures 5; # consecutive failures before the circuit breaker opens (default: 5)
    program\-user "nobody"; # optional privilege drop for plugin process (default: options { user })
    disable\-auto\-tc\-flag no; # yes: send oversized UDP replies as\-is (default: no)
    program\-reload\-line no; # yes: a reload writes the line "RELOAD" to the plugin (default: no)
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR \fBDesign Boundaries and Processing Semantics:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPre-filtering & Packet Validation\fR: KariDNS enforces standard basic DNS
header validation (QDCOUNT, valid OPCODES, EDNS version <= 0, valid QCLASS)
prior to dispatching queries to the program plugin. Corrupted queries that
violate fundamental DNS framing are responded to directly by KariDNS
(e.g. FORMERR / NOTIMP / REFUSED) before reaching the plugin.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUDP Truncation (\fBdisable\-auto\-tc\-flag\fB)\fR: With the default \fBno\fR,
a plugin reply larger than the UDP limit (EDNS UDP payload size, capped by the
server's UDP buffer size; 512 without EDNS) is replaced by a TC=1 reply with an
empty answer. With \fByes\fR, KariDNS sends the plugin reply as-is (up to 65,535
bytes), so the plugin itself is responsible for setting TC=1 on UDP.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTCP & AXFR Semantics\fR: TCP queries (including \fBAXFR\fR / \fBIXFR\fR) sent
to a program zone are forwarded directly to the plugin as a single
query-response transaction. Multi-envelope streaming AXFR is not supported. A
transfer request that is not shaped like one (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.1, RFC 1995
\[u00A7]3) is answered FORMERR, and one of a class other than IN REFUSED (EDE
20), before the plugin is asked (see "The class of a transfer
request"). \fBallow\-transfer\fR does not apply to a program zone: whether an
AXFR or IXFR is answered is the plugin's decision. It gets the client address
with every query and can refuse (REFUSED) whom it does not want to serve the
whole zone to.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSecurity & Isolation\fR: Plugin processes are started by the manager process
(outside the Capsicum sandbox, as \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR is) and drop
privileges (\fBprogram\-user\fR). The manager hands the backend the plugin's
stdin, its stdout and its process descriptor over a socket pair made before the
backend was forked; the backend limits each descriptor at once
(\fBcap_rights_limit()\fR: write, read, \fBpdkill()\fR) and treats every message
of the manager as untrusted. The path, the arguments and the user of a plugin
come from the manager's own reading of \fBkaridns.conf\fR, never from the
backend. All internal control channels, frontend IPC, and network sockets are
strictly closed via \fBclosefrom(3)\fR before executing the plugin. The plugin
starts with an empty environment (only \fBTZ\fR of the server is kept). A
\fBprogram\-user\fR that resolves to uid 0 or whose primary group is gid 0 is
refused (by \fBkaricheck conf\fR, at start, and by the manager whenever a reload
or the start-up asks it for a zone's plugin). At most 256 zones of type
\fBprogram\fR can be configured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRestarts\fR: a plugin that exits, or that is killed at
\fBprogram\-max\-failures\fR, is started again after 1, 2, 4, ... 60 s (back to
1 s after 10 minutes of running), the backoff of \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR; its
zone answers SERVFAIL meanwhile. The manager starts at most 4 plugins per second
once a burst of 256 is used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe \fBRELOAD\fB line\fR: with \fBprogram\-reload\-line yes;\fR a reload
(\fBkarictl reload\fR / \fBreconfig\fR, \fBSIGHUP\fR) writes the line
\fBRELOAD\fR to the zone's plugin, between two queries, and waits for no answer:
the plugin reads its own data again. Without it (the default) the line is never
sent, so a plugin written to the earlier protocol, which may take an unknown
line as an error, keeps working.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReload\fR (\fBSIGHUP\fR, \fBkarictl reload\fR / \fBreconfig\fR): a program
zone the reload adds gets its plugin; a zone whose \fBprogram\fR,
\fBprogram\-args\fR, \fBprogram\-user\fR, \fBprogram\-timeout\fR,
\fBprogram\-max\-failures\fR, \fBdisable\-auto\-tc\-flag\fR or
\fBprogram\-reload\-line\fR changed gets a new plugin, and the old one answers
until the new one runs and is then stopped; the plugin of a removed zone is
stopped; a plugin whose settings did not change is left alone (it gets the
\fBRELOAD\fR line with \fBprogram\-reload\-line yes;\fR). \fBkarictl reload
<zone>\fR is for the one zone, from the active configuration, and does not leave
the plugin alone: new settings get a new plugin; the same settings with
\fBprogram\-reload\-line yes;\fR get the \fBRELOAD\fR line; the same settings
without it get a new plugin with the same settings, which replaces the old one
in the same way (a plugin without the line can read its data again only by
starting again). The reply says which of the three happened. A plugin the
manager refuses to start (the configuration file changed again, a user it cannot
look up, no memory) is asked for again after 1, 2, 4, ... 60 s and at the next
reload; a refused replacement leaves the running plugin serving. None of this
needs a restart.
.PP
For full architectural details, IPC wire specifications, and complete runnable
examples in Perl, Python, C, Rust, and Go, see \fBKariDNS: Complete Guide
to 'type program' Zones\fR.
.RE
.SH FORWARD ZONES
KariDNS supports forwarding queries for specific zones to designated upstream
nameservers (\fBtype forward;\fR).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
zone "corp.example.com." {
    type forward;
    forwarders { 192.0.2.53; 198.51.100.53 port 5353; };
    # forwarders port 5353 { 192.0.2.53; };   the port of the list is the default of its addresses (without any: options { port }, else 53)
    forward\-timeout 2000; # total time budget in milliseconds for all forwarders (default: 2000)
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR \fBForward Zone Processing Semantics:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTransparent Query Relaying\fR: KariDNS does not maintain zone resource
records locally for forward zones. Incoming queries matching the zone are
forwarded to the configured \fBforwarders\fR list in order, failing over to the
next forwarder within the shared \fBforward\-timeout\fR budget. Each forwarder
gets the remaining time divided by the number of forwarders not tried yet (with
two forwarders and the default 2000 ms: 1000 ms each, the second one also gets
what the first one did not use). A reply whose ID, QR bit or question does not
match is discarded and the wait for that forwarder continues (RFC 5452
\[u00A7]9.1). The fallback to TCP after a truncated reply, including its
connection setup, uses the time that is still left.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNo Subprocess Overhead\fR: Unlike \fBtype program\fR zones, forward zones do
not spawn external processes and do not require global opt-in flags like
\fBallow\-program\-zones\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBImmediate Reload Support\fR: Changes to \fBforwarders\fR or
\fBforward\-timeout\fR take effect immediately upon configuration reload
(\fBSIGHUP\fR / \fBkarictl reload\fR) without requiring a full server restart.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUnsupported Operations\fR: Dynamic Update (RFC 2136) and \fBNOTIFY\fR
requests are not supported on forward zones and are rejected with \fBNOTIMP\fR;
a zone transfer (\fBAXFR\fR/\fBIXFR\fR, TCP or UDP) is REFUSED with EDE 20 (Not
Authoritative), as for a zone this server does not have.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSecurity & Transaction ID Randomization\fR: When relaying to upstream
forwarders, KariDNS assigns a fresh cryptographic random transaction ID
(\fBarc4random\fR) and verifies that the upstream response Question section and
ID match before relaying the answer with the client's original transaction ID
restored.
.RE
.SH POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS (milestone B9a)
KariDNS reads, serves and transfers the two record types that PowerDNS adds to
the DNS: \fBALIAS\fR (a name that stands for the addresses of another name, the
"ANAME" idea) and \fBLUA\fR (a record whose answer is the result of a Lua
snippet). They are not IETF types. PowerDNS stores them as private-use types of
CLASS IN and sends them like that in a zone transfer (RFC 6895 \[u00A7]3.1):
ALIAS is \fBTYPE65401\fR (RDATA = the target name, uncompressed, RFC 3597
\[u00A7]4) and LUA is \fBTYPE65402\fR (RDATA = the 16-bit type of the answer,
then the code as \fB<character\-string>\fRs). KariDNS keeps exactly that form,
so an AXFR out is byte for byte what PowerDNS sends
(\fBoutgoing\-axfr\-expand\-alias=no\fR), and a zone transferred in from
PowerDNS keeps its ALIAS and LUA records.
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR The Extended AXFR records of KariDNS use the same two type numbers
(65401, 65402) in \fBCLASS 65302\fR. Everything that reads or writes a transfer
tells them apart by the pair (type, class), never by the type alone: a record of
CLASS IN is a zone record, whatever its type number; a record of CLASS 65302 is
an Extended AXFR record, whatever it holds.
.RE
.PP
\fBZone files.\fR \fBALIAS <target>\fR and \fBLUA <type> "<code>" ["<more
code>" ...]\fR are accepted next to the generic forms (\fBTYPE65401 \e#
15 ...\fR, RFC 3597 \[u00A7]5). A relative target is relative to the origin. The
code of a LUA record is cut into \fB<character\-string>\fRs of at most 255
octets, as PowerDNS does. In a PowerDNS database the rows are \fBtype = ALIAS\fR
with the target in \fBcontent\fR (\fBt.target.example.\fR) and \fBtype = LUA\fR
with \fBA ";return {'192.0.2.1'}"\fR. \fBtests/db/zone2sql\fR writes these rows.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
@    300 IN ALIAS lb.target.example.      ; A / AAAA of the apex from another name (RFC 1034 \[u00A7]3.6.2 forbids a CNAME here)
www  300 IN ALIAS lb.target.example.
txt  300 IN LUA   TXT ";return 'hello'"   ; kept for transfers, never answered
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBZone rules\fR (a zone that breaks them is not loaded; \fBkaricheck zone\fR
and \fBkaricheck zones\fR report them; a database zone that breaks them is
rejected with the reason in the log and in \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR; an incoming
transfer that breaks them is refused):
.IP \(bu 2
an ALIAS name must not also own an A or AAAA RRset (the ALIAS stands for
them). TXT, MX, NS and the other types may coexist;
.IP \(bu 2
a zone with an ALIAS must not hold DNSSEC records (DNSKEY, RRSIG, NSEC, NSEC3,
NSEC3PARAM), and a database zone with \fBPRESIGNED\fR metadata must not hold an
ALIAS: an expanded answer cannot be signed offline. KariDNS does no online
signing;
.IP \(bu 2
the RDATA of both types must be well formed (one uncompressed name; a type and
\fB<character\-string>\fRs that fill the RDATA);
.IP \(bu 2
\fBENABLE\-LUA\-RECORDS\fR metadata with the value \fB1\fR rejects the database
zone (LUA records are not run, so enabling them would silently change the
answers); \fB0\fR or no row is fine.
.SS Default behaviour (no alias-resolver)
This is PowerDNS with \fBexpand\-alias=no\fR and \fBenable\-lua\-records=no\fR,
and the answers are the same (checked against PowerDNS 5.1.4,
\fBtests/run_db_alias_lua_test.sh\fR):
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Query	\&Answer
_
T{
A or AAAA (or any other type the name does not own) at an ALIAS name
T}	\&NOERROR, no data
T{
\fBTYPE65401\fR at an ALIAS name, and \fBANY\fR there
T}	T{
the ALIAS RR (\fBANY\fR lists it with the other RRsets)
T}
T{
ALIAS at a wildcard, a name below it
T}	T{
the ALIAS RR for \fBTYPE65401\fR and \fBANY\fR, no data for A and AAAA (an ALIAS
RR is a plain RR in this mode)
T}
T{
anything at a name that owns \fBonly LUA\fR records, including \fBTYPE65402\fR
and \fBANY\fR
T}	T{
NXDOMAIN (as if the name did not exist; a wildcard that holds only LUA records
is not there either)
T}
T{
\fBANY\fR or \fBTYPE65402\fR at a name with LUA and other records
T}	T{
\fBANY\fR lists the other RRsets only; \fBTYPE65402\fR gets no data
T}
\&AXFR / IXFR, Extended AXFR	T{
every ALIAS and LUA RR, as TYPE65401 / TYPE65402
T}
.TE
.PP
A name that owns LUA records and descendants is an empty non-terminal like any
other (NOERROR, no data).
.SS Expansion (alias-resolver)
.RS 2
.nf
options { alias\-resolver { 192.0.2.53; 2001:db8::53 port 5353; }; };   # every zone
zone "example.test." { ...; alias\-resolver { 192.0.2.54; }; };         # this zone only (replaces the options value)
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBalias\-resolver\fR names 1 to 8 resolvers (IP addresses, never names). A
zone's list replaces the options list; for the zones of the database the order
is the zone's settings text (or its profile), then \fBoptions { alias\-resolver
}\fR, then \fBoptions { database { alias\-resolver } }\fR. It works for file
zones, transferred zones and database zones alike. A statement may be written in
the settings text of a database zone and in a profile (it is not sent with
Extended AXFR: a resolver address is local to the server). A configuration
reload takes effect at once.
.PP
With it, A and AAAA at an ALIAS name are answered with the addresses the
resolver gives for the target, as PowerDNS does with \fBexpand\-alias=yes\fR and
\fBresolver=...\fR:
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Query	\&Answer
_
\&A, AAAA	T{
the addresses of the target (the CNAME chain of the resolver's answer is
followed, at most 8 hops); NOERROR with no data when the target has none
T}
\&\fBANY\fR	T{
the expanded A and AAAA only (PowerDNS answers \fBANY\fR at an ALIAS name with
these two and leaves the other RRsets out)
T}
\&\fBTYPE65401\fR	T{
no data: the ALIAS RR is not an answer any more (AXFR still carries it)
T}
T{
a target that cannot be resolved: the resolver does not answer, or answers
SERVFAIL, REFUSED or NXDOMAIN, a CNAME loop or a chain longer than 8
T}	T{
SERVFAIL (with EDE 22 when the client sent EDNS and \fBsend\-extended\-errors\fR
is on)
T}
\&a wildcard ALIAS	T{
not expanded (as in PowerDNS 5.1.4): no data for A and AAAA, the ALIAS RR for
\fBANY\fR
T}
T{
the glue of an MX / NS / SRV target that is an ALIAS name
T}	\&none
.TE
.PP
\fBHow it is done.\fR The Backend never waits for a resolver. One auxiliary
thread resolves every ALIAS target of the published zones in advance, through
the same broker path as the forwarders of a \fBtype forward\fR zone and the
transfers of a secondary (no socket call is added to the sandboxed Backend): a
UDP query with RD=1 and EDNS0, TCP when the answer is truncated; the answer must
come back on the connected socket with the right ID and the question asked, and
RCODE NOERROR (RFC 5452 \[u00A7]9.1). The thread publishes the results as one
immutable table that queries only read (the table is replaced and the old one
freed after a grace period, like zone versions). Answers that carry expanded
addresses are never put into the shared response cache. The resolver is trusted:
nothing is DNSSEC-validated.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTTL.\fR The TTL of an expanded answer is the smaller of the ALIAS record's
TTL and what is left of the TTL of the resolver's answer (the TTL of the final A
/ AAAA RRset, as PowerDNS takes it; CNAME TTLs are not mixed in), counting down,
so a downstream cache never keeps the data longer than the resolver
allowed. (PowerDNS gives the upstream TTL as it is, without the ALIAS record's
TTL and without counting down.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRefresh.\fR A target is queried again after two thirds of its TTL and at
least 5 seconds after the last time (an upstream TTL of 0 gives a 5 second
refresh and TTL 0 answers). A new zone version keeps the results of the targets
it still has; a target that no zone has any more is dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFailure.\fR A target that has \fBnever\fR been resolved (a zone that was just
loaded, or a resolver that is down since the start) answers SERVFAIL until the
first resolution works; PowerDNS resolves at the first query, so the first
answers after a load may be SERVFAIL where PowerDNS would wait. A target that
\fBwas\fR resolved keeps its last result when a refresh fails: it is served for
up to one day after its TTL ran out, with TTL 30 (RFC 8767 \[u00A7]4, \[u00A7]5;
the answer is the last known data, not an error), and the refresh is retried in
the background after 5 s, 10 s, 20 s ... up to 320 s. After a day without a
success the target answers SERVFAIL. A resolver set that does not answer at all
is left alone for 10 s between tries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBounds.\fR At most 1,024 ALIAS records per zone are looked at (the rest
answer SERVFAIL, logged), 16,384 jobs in all (a target is two jobs: A and AAAA),
32 addresses per type, 64 different resolver lists, 20 upstream queries per 200
ms tick (about 100 per second) and 3 s for one resolution. The results are about
100 octets each.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLogs.\fR The first failure of a target and its recovery are logged once
(\fB[Alias] ...\fR in syslog).
.PP
Differences from PowerDNS, on purpose: the TTL rule above; the stale answers and
the "never resolved" SERVFAIL window; the SOA record that every NODATA answer
carries (RFC 2308 \[u00A7]2.2; PowerDNS 5.1.4 sends none with the NODATA of an
expansion); a truncated answer of the resolver is retried over TCP (PowerDNS
5.1.4 takes it for an answer without data); an ALIAS beside A / AAAA and an
ALIAS in a signed zone are zone errors; \fBENABLE\-LUA\-RECORDS=1\fR is
refused. LUA records themselves are \fBnot\fR run: this release implements what
PowerDNS does with \fBenable\-lua\-records=no\fR (design decision D13).
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR Dynamic Update (RFC 2136) works on zones that hold ALIAS records,
but an update that would put an A or AAAA RR at an ALIAS name is refused with
the rest of the zone check (SERVFAIL), and the "name is in use" prerequisite
counts a name that holds only LUA records as in use although queries get
NXDOMAIN.
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR Several servers that share one PostgreSQL database answer the A and
AAAA queries at an ALIAS name from their own resolver and at their own moment,
so those answers can differ between the servers (zone transfers and SOA serials
do not); LUA records are never run and so are the same. See "Several servers as
primaries" under the PostgreSQL driver, and \fBkaricheck conf\fR, which warns
about \fBserver\-id\fR together with \fBalias\-resolver\fR.
.RE
.SH ZONE FILE FORMAT
The default \fBfile\-format\fR is the master file format of RFC 1035 \[u00A7]5
as BIND reads it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRecords\fR: \fB<owner> [<TTL>] [<class>] <type> <RDATA>\fR; TTL and class may
appear in either order, each once. PowerDNS's \fBALIAS <target>\fR and \fBLUA
<type> "<code>"\fR are accepted too (see POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS). Type
and class mnemonics are case-insensitive (\fBin a\fR, \fBIN Mx\fR). Unknown
types and classes use the RFC 3597 forms \fBTYPEnn\fR, \fBCLASSnn\fR and \fB\e#
<length> <hex>\fR. The generic form of a type the server knows must carry RDATA
that is valid for that type (RFC 3597 \[u00A7]5): \fBPTR \e# 0\fR, \fBNS \e# 3
010203\fR or a compressed name in the hex are zone file errors, reported by
\fBkaricheck zone\fR at the record's line. The same rule applies to the generic
\fB:\fR lines of a tinydns data file (\fB:name:type:rdata\fR):
\fB:x.example:1:\e001\fR (an A of one octet) is an error at that line. tinydns
itself stores any octets there; KariDNS used to serve such RDATA as it
was. Zero-length RDATA is legal for a type without a defined format
(\fBTYPE65534 \e# 0\fR) and is transferred (AXFR/IXFR) and served like any other
record.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\e# <length> <hex>\fB\fR (RFC 3597 \[u00A7]5): the hex digits may be split
over any number of tokens, for every type, as far as the limit of a record line
allows (512 fields; a line with more is a zone file error, use \fB( )\fR and
fewer, longer tokens). The tokens are the RDATA octets whatever the type's own
text form is, so the same record with 3 tokens or with 100 is the same record.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLong strings\fR: a \fB<character\-string>\fR of a TXT-like type (\fBTXT\fR,
\fBSPF\fR, \fBAVC\fR, \fBNINFO\fR, \fBRESINFO\fR, \fBWALLET\fR) over 255 octets
is cut into 255-octet strings (RFC 1035 \[u00A7]3.3.14 allows several strings),
which BIND 9.20 does not do: it refuses the zone. \fBkaridns\fR serves and
transfers the split form without a message; \fBkaricheck zone\fR warns with the
file and line (\fBa character\-string of more than 255 octets ... BIND rejects
this\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSingleton types\fR: \fBRESINFO\fR (RFC 9606 \[u00A7]3; BIND marks it
\fBDNS_RDATATYPEATTR_SINGLETON\fR) may have only one record at a name. Two
records with different RDATA at one name refuse the zone (BIND: \fBmultiple RRs
of singleton type\fR, \fBDNS_R_SINGLETON\fR from \fBlib/dns/rdataslab.c\fR) in a
zone file, in a transfer (AXFR/IXFR: the zone keeps its old data and the error
is logged), in a database zone and in \fBkaricheck zone\fR /
\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-check\fR; the same RDATA twice is one record. A dynamic
update that adds a second, different one to a name fails with SERVFAIL and
changes nothing (BIND \fBlib/ns/update.c\fR \fBreplaces_p()\fR replaces only
CNAME, DNAME, SOA, NSEC, RRSIG, WKS and NSEC3PARAM, so RESINFO is not replaced:
delete the old record in the same update to replace it).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTypes with an unusual text form\fR (as BIND 9.20 reads them; no name
compression in any of them, RFC 3597 \[u00A7]4): \fBRESINFO\fR (261, RFC 9606)
and \fBWALLET\fR (262, IANA registry) are one or more
\fB<character\-string>\fRs, written and displayed like \fBTXT\fR (BIND
\fBgeneric_fromtext_txt()\fR); \fBEID\fR (31) and \fBNIMLOC\fR (32) are hex
digits in one or more tokens (\fBEID 0102 0304\fR), an odd number of digits or a
non-hex character is a zone file error, and they are displayed as one hex
string. \fBNULL\fR (10) and the obsolete \fBUINFO\fR (100), \fBUID\fR (101),
\fBGID\fR (102) and \fBUNSPEC\fR (103) have a name but no text form in BIND
9.18/9.20 (\fBgen.c\fR only registers the names): they are accepted only as
\fB\e# <length> <hex>\fR (a text form is a zone file error) and \fBdag\fR shows
them in that form.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCase of names\fR: owner names and the domain names inside RDATA are answered
and transferred with the letters as written (RFC 4343 \[u00A7]4.1), for every
type, from a zone file and from a database alike. The only exception is name
compression (types NS, CNAME, PTR, MX, SOA, MINFO and the owner): a name that is
compressed against an earlier name of the same message that differs only in case
shows the case of the earlier one, as RFC 4343 \[u00A7]4.1 allows. The
lower-case form of RFC 4034 \[u00A7]6.2 is used only where DNSSEC needs the
canonical form (ZONEMD digests, UPDATE and IXFR comparisons, \fBkaricheck\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBClasses\fR: \fBIN\fR (the default when the class is omitted), \fBCH\fR,
\fBHS\fR and \fBCLASSnn\fR are kept with the record. The server answers queries
of class IN and CH; records of other classes are loaded but not answered
(queries of those classes are REFUSED). \fBNONE\fR, \fBANY\fR, \fBCLASS0\fR and
class 65302 (used internally by KariDNS) are errors. \fBA zone has one class\fR,
the class of its first record (a record without a class has it): a later record
of another class is a zone file error at its line, with BIND's text
(\fBlib/dns/master.c\fR) \fBclass 'CH' != zone class 'IN'\fR (\fBclass 'IN' !=
zone class 'CH'\fR when the first record is a CH one). The same holds for the
records of \fB$INCLUDE\fR files and \fB$GENERATE\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOmitted TTL\fR (same order as BIND): the TTL on the record; else \fB$TTL\fR
(RFC 2308 \[u00A7]4); else the last TTL written explicitly on a record (RFC 1035
\[u00A7]5.1, also across \fB$INCLUDE\fR); else, for an SOA record, its MINIMUM
field, which then acts like \fB$TTL\fR; else 3600. BIND rejects such a zone ("no
TTL specified"); here the zone loads, the server logs a warning with the file
and line of the first such record and the number of records that got 3600
(\fB<file>:<line>: no TTL specified for '<owner>' ...\fR; an \fB$INCLUDE\fR file
is named by its own path), and \fBkaricheck zone\fR / \fBkaricheck conf\fR warn
in the same words.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTTL notation\fR (BIND 9.20 ARM 3.5.3 and 3.5.5.4: "Valid TTLs are of the
range 0-2147483647 seconds"): digits with \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBd\fR,
\fBw\fR units in any letter case, e.g. \fB3600\fR, \fB1h30m\fR, \fB1w2d\fR; as
in BIND (lib/dns/ttl.c \fBbind_ttl()\fR) a number without a unit is accepted
only on its own (\fB30\fR), not after a unit (\fB1h30\fR is an error), and the
text may be at most 63 characters long. A \fB$TTL\fR or record TTL that is not
in that notation (\fB1x\fR, \fBabc\fR, a sign, \fB1h\-5\fR) or above 2147483647
is a zone file error at that position, as is a \fB$TTL\fR without a value. It
used to be read as 3600 without a word. The SOA refresh, retry, expire and
minimum fields use the same notation (up to 4294967295) and are errors when
malformed. \fBThe SOA serial\fR must be a plain decimal number 0-4294967295
(leading zeros allowed): BIND 9.20 reads it as a number token
(lib/dns/rdata/generic/soa_6.c \fBfromtext_soa()\fR, lib/isc/lex.c,
lib/isc/parseint.c) and rejects the zone for a sign, a unit, a hex prefix, an
empty value or a value above 32 bits; here such a serial is a zone file error
(\fBInvalid SOA serial ...\fR / \fBSOA serial out of range ...\fR) at its
position, also in the \fBZ\fR line of a tinydns data file (an empty tinydns
serial still means the time stamp of the data file). It used to be read as 0 or
cut to 32 bits. A TTL above 2147483647 in a transferred or database record is
still served as 2147483647 (RFC 8767 \[u00A7]4).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB$ORIGIN <name>\fB\fR and the origin argument of \fB\fB$INCLUDE <file>
[<origin>]\fB\fR: a name without a trailing dot is relative to the current
origin (RFC 1035 \[u00A7]5.1). \fB$INCLUDE\fR does not change the origin of the
including file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB$INCLUDE\fB paths\fR (BIND 9.20 ARM 3.5.5.3: "The filename can be an
absolute path, or a relative path"): a relative name is read from \fBoptions {
directory }\fR when it is set (BIND: named's working directory), otherwise from
the directory of the zone file. The server reads files in capability mode and
can open only directories that it opened when it started, so the file must lie
inside one of two trees: the directory of the zone file, or \fBdirectory\fR. An
absolute path, or a path that leaves the base through \fB..\fR, is accepted only
when the normalized path (no symbolic link is looked at) stays inside one of
those trees; any other is a zone file error, for example
\fB$INCLUDE '/etc/hosts' is outside the directories opened at startup (zone file
dir, directory): the sandboxed server can read only below them\fR. \fBkaricheck
zone\fR applies the same rules and gives the same message. A reload may bring in
a file in a sub-directory that did not exist at start-up, as long as it is below
one of those trees.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB$GENERATE <range> <lhs> [<TTL>] [<class>] <type> <rhs>\fB\fR (BIND
syntax): \fB<range>\fR is \fBstart\-stop[/step]\fR (at most 100,000
records). For each value, \fB$\fR in \fB<lhs>\fR and \fB<rhs>\fR is replaced by
the value, \fB${offset[,width[,base]]}\fR with base \fBd\fR, \fBo\fR, \fBx\fR,
\fBX\fR, \fBn\fR or \fBN\fR (\fBn\fR/\fBN\fR: nibbles in reverse order separated
by dots, as for \fBip6.arpa\fR; the width counts the dots), \fB$$\fR gives
\fB$\fR and \fB\e$\fR a literal \fB$\fR. Any RR type can be generated; the
resulting line is read like any other record line, so
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR/\fB$LOCATION\fR tags and the TTL rules apply. \fB<rhs>\fR is
one field: quote RDATA that contains spaces, e.g. \fB$GENERATE 1\-10 host$ MX
"10 mail$"\fR. The quotes only keep the field together; the text inside is split
on blanks again, so one TXT character-string that contains a blank cannot be
generated (BIND keeps \fBTXT "a b"\fR as one string): put such records in the
zone file itself.
.SH TINYDNS ZONE FORMAT
KariDNS can load zone data directly from djbdns/tinydns-style plain-text
\fBdata\fR files (not the compiled \fBdata.cdb\fR), in addition to standard
BIND-style zone files.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
zone "example.com." {
    type master;
    file "/usr/local/etc/karidns/data/example.com.tinydns";
    file\-format tinydns;   # omit for the default "bind" format
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
A single \fBdata\fR file that mixes forward-zone records (e.g. \fB=host:ip\fR)
and reverse-zone records (\fBin\-addr.arpa.\fR) can be referenced from multiple
\fBzone {}\fR blocks simultaneously; each zone automatically keeps only the
records belonging to it (longest-suffix match against all configured zone names,
so parent/child zone delegation is handled correctly without duplicate records).
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR \fBNumeric fields and lines that cannot be kept:\fR the tinydns
parser does not limit the 8-, 16- and 32-bit numeric fields of the extension
lines (\fBS\fR, \fBN\fR, \fB_\fR, ...). djbdns itself truncates such a value;
KariDNS keeps it, and a record whose value does not fit its field is left out
with a warning when the zone is loaded (\fBkaricheck\fR reports it as
\fBzone.record.wire\fR). An SSHFP line whose fingerprint is not hexadecimal is
left out as well; \fBkaricheck\fR reports it once per file
(\fBzone.tinydns.skipped\fR).
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR \fBtinydns Format Support Scope:\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBVerified against djbdns 1.05 source\fR: Record types \fB.\fR \fB&\fR \fB+\fR
\fB=\fR \fB\-\fR \fB@\fR \fB'\fR \fB^\fR \fBC\fR \fBZ\fR \fB:\fR are supported,
including exact default TTL values, the \fBx\fR (nameserver/MX target) expansion
rules, 127-byte TXT character-string chunking, and the same lenient IPv4 octet
parsing (no range validation, trailing garbage tolerated) as the original
\fBtinydns\-data\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBExtensions\fR (not in djbdns 1.05). Empty numeric fields are 0; the default
TTL is 86400; \fBtimestamp\fR and \fBlo\fR work as for the other types:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fB3fqdn:ip6:ttl:timestamp:lo\fR: AAAA. \fBip6\fR is 32 hex digits without
colons (e.g. \fB20010db8000000000000000000000001\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB6fqdn:ip6:ttl:timestamp:lo\fR: AAAA plus PTR records under \fBip6.arpa\fR and
\fBip6.int\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSfqdn:ip:x:port:weight:priority:ttl:timestamp:lo\fR: SRV with target \fBx\fR
(a full name, no expansion), plus an A record for \fBx\fR when \fBip\fR is
given.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNfqdn:order:pref:flags:service:regexp:replacement:ttl:timestamp:lo\fR:
NAPTR. \fBflags\fR, \fBservice\fR and \fBregexp\fR take the octal escapes of
\fB:\fR records.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB_fqdn:algorithm:fptype:fingerprint:ttl:timestamp:lo\fR: SSHFP with a hex
fingerprint; a line whose fingerprint is not valid hex is skipped.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBtimestamp\fB field\fR: Supported with \fBreal-time query evaluation\fR,
identical to original djbdns behavior. A record whose \fBtimestamp\fR is in the
future is excluded dynamically on every query (not just at load time). The
\fBttl=0\fR "countdown TTL" variant is likewise computed on every query: the
remaining seconds until the timestamp are clamped to [2, 3600] and served as the
record TTL.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB%\fB location (split-horizon by client IP)\fR: Supported natively during
query resolution. KariDNS compiles \fB%<loc>:<prefix>\fR location lines and
trailing \fB:loc\fR record fields into memory, performing bitwise longest-prefix
matching against the querying client's source IPv4 address with zero heap
allocation on the hot path. As an extension, \fB<prefix>\fR may end in \fB/n\fR
(\fB/0\fR\[u2013]\fB/32\fR) to give the prefix length in bits,
e.g. \fB%in:10.0.0.0/8\fR.
.RE
.SS Several SOA records, and a client that sees none (milestone B8t-1)
A zone can have one SOA record per client group: a \fBZ\fR line per location in
a tinydns data file (the format allows it: \fBtinydns\-data.c\fR stores the SOA
like every record, with its location and time stamp), or \fB$LOCATION\fR /
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR\-tagged SOA records in a zone file. Several SOA records that
every client sees stay an error (\fBkaricheck\fR). The rules, for a zone file
and for a database zone alike:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOne serial.\fR All SOA records of the zone have the same serial (RFC 2181
\[u00A7]6.1: one SOA per zone, so one zone serial). A zone that breaks this is
not loaded: a reload logs \fBzone '...' not loaded from '...': the SOA records
of zone '...' have different serials (...)\fR and the version in service stays;
\fBkaricheck\fR reports an error. A zone that holds DNSSEC records (RRSIG,
DNSKEY, NSEC, NSEC3) cannot have several or restricted SOA records either: the
signature of the SOA RRset would not hold for every client.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReference SOA.\fR The SOA record without a location, time stamp or tag if
there is one, else the first in file (row) order. Everything that is zone-wide
comes from it: the serial (SOA queries of a secondary, NOTIFY, IXFR, the serial
that Dynamic Update raises in all of them), refresh / retry / expire, and the
EDNS EXPIRE value (RFC 7314).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAnswers.\fR The SOA a client gets is the first SOA it can see, with the rules
that decide the other records (its tinydns location, its \fB$LOCATION\fR /
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR tag, the tinydns time stamp and countdown, as
\fBtdlookup.c\fR does). A query for the SOA (or ANY) at the apex gets that one
SOA only. NXDOMAIN and NODATA carry it in the authority section (RFC 2308
\[u00A7]3) and its MINIMUM is the negative TTL (RFC 2308 \[u00A7]5).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA client that sees no SOA\fR of the zone, whether or not it sees an NS
record, gets \fBREFUSED with AA=0 and Extended DNS Error 18 (Prohibited, RFC
8914 \[u00A7]4.19)\fR, for every name and type of the zone and for a zone
transfer. This is the answer of BIND 9.20 to a client a zone is not served to
(no matching view, \fBallow\-query\fR denial); \fBtdlookup.c\fR differs: with a
visible NS it answers a referral without AA, and with nothing visible it sends
no response. The zone is treated as not served to that client: the closest zone
decides, there is no fall back to the answer of a parent zone (a parent's
referral to the child is not shown), and a CNAME chain that leads into such a
zone ends at the CNAME.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStandard AXFR and IXFR\fR (a request without the KariDNS extension, e.g. from
BIND, NSD or \fBdig\fR) of a zone with per-client records is the zone \fBas the
requester sees it now\fR, as \fBaxfrdns.c\fR builds it: the RRs the requester's
address does not match (tinydns location, \fB$LOCATION\fR tag) or that are not
active (time stamp) are not sent, a record with a countdown TTL is sent with TTL
2, no location, tag or time stamp is sent, an \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR\-tagged RR is
not sent (a transfer has no client subnet), the opening and closing SOA is the
first SOA the requester sees, and a requester that sees none is refused
(REFUSED, AA=0, EDE 18). Every other SOA record is left out of the body. A zone
without such RRs is transferred as before. (Before this release a standard
transfer left out every restricted RR, and it kept all SOA records.) An IXFR of
such a zone is answered with the whole zone, as RFC 1995 \[u00A7]4 allows,
unless the requester is up to date (then one SOA).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBExtended AXFR of such a zone (milestone B8t-2)\fR is the whole zone, for a
KariDNS secondary that speaks version 3 (see "Secondary zones: Extended AXFR
version 3 and the settings of the primary"): the frame is the reference SOA,
every SOA record travels in the body with its attributes, and the secondary ends
up with exactly the SOA set of the primary and answers each client as the
primary does. A secondary of version 2 is refused (REFUSED with a text): sending
it the reference SOA alone would show a hidden SOA to everybody. A KariDNS
secondary outside every SOA's location can still refresh: the extension makes
the primary answer with the reference SOA whatever the requester's address is (a
SOA or IXFR question that carries the extension option for the zone gets it
too).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTTLs of an RRset (milestone B8t-2).\fR The TTLs of the RRs of one name and
type are brought to the lowest one (RFC 2181 \[u00A7]5.2) only among RRs that
are seen by the same clients: no location / tag / time stamp, or the same. An RR
that only some clients see keeps the TTL it was written with, also when an RR
that everybody sees has the same name and type: a client that sees both gets an
RRset whose RRs have different TTLs, as it does from tinydns
(\fB+www:192.0.2.1:300\fR and \fB+www:192.0.2.2:60::in\fR). \fBkaricheck\fR
warns for that case (an unrestricted and a restricted RR of one name and type
with different TTLs). A zone without such RRs is unchanged. The rule is the same
for zone files, database zones and zones received from a primary.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCache.\fR A zone with restricted RRs is never served from the response cache,
so the answer cannot be shared between clients.
.PP
The reference run against djbdns 1.05
(\fBtests/run_tinydns_soa_location_test.sh\fR with \fBDJBDNS_DIR\fR) shows the
same SOA for each location, the same NXDOMAIN and the same transfer records; the
difference is the client that sees no SOA (above).
.SH CLIENT GEOLOCATION & SUBNET STEERING (ECS & LOCATION)
KariDNS provides high-performance, record-level split-horizon response steering
across both BIND and tinydns zone formats:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBIND Zone \fB$LOCATION\fB & \fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fB\fR: Steers responses based
on the querying client's immediate socket IP address (IPv4 and IPv6). Tags can
be defined directly in zone files (\fB$LOCATION\-TAG <tag> <cidrs>\fR) or in
\fBkaridns.conf\fR (\fBlocation\-tags\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBIND Zone \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fB & \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fB\fR: Steers responses
based on EDNS0 Client Subnet (ECS, RFC 7871) options supplied by trusted
recursive resolvers (\fBecs\-enable yes;\fR and
\fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR). Tags can be defined in zone files
(\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR) or in \fBkaridns.conf\fR (\fBecs\-tags\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtinydns \fBlocation\fB (\fB%\fB)\fR: Steers responses based on client IPv4
longest-prefix matching declared with \fB%<loc>:<prefix>\fR lines and trailing
\fB:loc\fR record tags.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSeveral SOA records per zone (B8t-1)\fR: a SOA can carry a tinydns location
or time stamp, or a \fB$LOCATION\fR / \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR tag; a client gets the
first SOA it sees, and a client that sees none is refused. See "Several SOA
records, and a client that sees none" in the tinydns section.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTTLs of records that only some clients see (B8t-2)\fR: the lowest-TTL rule
for an RRset (RFC 2181 \[u00A7]5.2) applies only among records with the same
location, tag and time stamp; a record with a location, tag or time stamp keeps
the TTL it was written with, also beside a record for everybody. \fBkaricheck\fR
warns when the two have different TTLs. See "Several SOA records, and a client
that sees none".
.IP \(bu 2
\fBKariDNS Extended AXFR (Option 65153)\fR: Replicates zone directives, tags,
the SOA set and (version 3) the primary's zone settings across KariDNS primary
and secondary servers, while providing a standard AXFR fallback (Plan B) for
non-KariDNS clients: the zone as the requester sees it. See "Secondary zones:
Extended AXFR version 3 and the settings of the primary".
.PP
For complete configuration syntax, query evaluation flows, and Extended AXFR
details, see \fBKariDNS: Client Geolocation & Subnet Steering Guide\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
\fB\-v\fR / \fB\-\-version\fR, or the server was stopped (\fBSIGTERM\fR,
\fBSIGINT\fR, \fBkarictl stop\fR).
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
The server did not start or stopped on an error: the configuration file cannot
be read or has errors, a port, log file, PID file or control socket cannot be
opened, or the server could not carry on.
.TP 8
\fB2\fR
Usage error: no configuration file was given, or the value of \fB\-p\fR is a
number outside 1-65535. The usage text goes to standard error (and syslog);
nothing was started. The tools \fBkaricheck\fR, \fBkarictl\fR and
\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR use 2 for usage errors in the same way; \fBdag\fR
follows \fBdig\fR instead.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP 8
\fBSIGHUP\fR
Same as \fBkarictl reconfig\fR: re-reads the configuration file and swaps it in
atomically (RCU). Zone files whose modification time and size have not changed
since they were loaded are not re-read; use \fBkarictl reload\fR to re-read all
zone files. Send it to the manager process (the PID in the PID file); the
manager forwards it to the backend. When it reaches every process (\fBpkill
\-HUP karidns\fR), the server still reloads once: the backend acts only on the
manager's forward, and a \fBSIGHUP\fR sent to the backend process alone is
logged and ignored.
.TP 8
\fBSIGTERM\fR, \fBSIGINT\fR
Sent to the manager process, stops all child processes (backend, frontend
routers, connect broker), removes the PID file and exits. A child that has not
ended 10 seconds after the SIGTERM it was sent is killed with SIGKILL (logged);
a second \fBSIGTERM\fR or \fBSIGINT\fR to the manager kills the remaining
children at once. The exit status is then non-zero.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 8
\fBTZ\fR
The time zone of the time stamps (\fBprint\-time local\fR); passed on to
\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR and the program zone plugins.
.TP 8
\fBKARIDNS_NOSYSLOG=1\fR
A test hook (0.7, in the spirit of BIND's \fBnamed \-T nosyslog\fR): every line
the server would give \fBsyslog(3)\fR -- the built-in \fBdefault_syslog\fR,
\fBsyslog\fR channels, and the messages written before the logging statement is
in force -- goes to standard error only, as \fBKariDNS <pid> \- \- <text>\fR,
the same copy that \fBopenlog(3)\fR's \fBLOG_PERROR\fR prints on standard error
anyway. The system log gets nothing; file and \fBstderr\fR channels are
unchanged. Only the value \fB1\fR counts. The integration tests export it
(\fBtests/lib_proc.sh\fR), so that the servers they start do not fill the build
host's \fB/var/log/messages\fR and still show their messages on the standard
error the tests read. Do not set it for a daemonized server: its standard error
is \fB/dev/null\fR, so those lines would be lost.
.TP 8
\fBKARIDNS_XFER_MINUTE_SECS=n\fR
A test hook: a "minute" of the transfer time limits is n seconds (1..60); see
\fBmax\-transfer\-time\-in\fR and the related options.
.SH FILES
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf\fR
Configuration file used by the FreeBSD \fBrc.d\fR script
(\fBkaridns_config\fR). The package installs \fBkaridns.conf.sample\fR next to
it.
.TP 8
\fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR
Default UNIX domain socket for control communication with \fBkarictl\fR.
.TP 8
\fB/var/run/karidns/karidns.pid\fR
Default process ID file when daemonized.
.TP 8
\fB<directory>/karidns.run\fR
The built-in \fBdefault_debug\fR channel (BIND's \fBnamed.run\fR), in \fBoptions
{ directory }\fR: created at the start, written while the debug level
(\fB\-d\fR, \fBkarictl trace\fR) is above 0. Not made without a directory. See
\fBlogging { ... }\fR.
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-dbloader\fR
The database loader (the packages \fBkaridns\-sqlite3\fR,
\fBkaridns\-postgresql\fR and \fBkaridns\-db\fR; the default
\fBloader\-path\fR).
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/bin/karidns\-zone\fR
The zone importer and exporter (the same packages; \fBkaridns\-zone(8)\fR).
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/\fR
The SQL files for the databases (\fBkaridns_ext.*.sql\fR,
\fBkaridns_ext_v1_to_v2.*.sql\fR, \fBkaridns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR,
\fBkaridns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR); \fBdb/\fR in a source tree. See
\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR.
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/share/doc/karidns/\fR
These manuals (\fBkaridns.md\fR, \fBkarictl.md\fR, \fBkaricheck.md\fR,
\fBdag.md\fR, \fBdag_replay.md\fR and, with the loader,
\fBkaridns\-dbloader.md\fR, \fBkaridns\-zone.md\fR and
\fBkaridns\-signer.md\fR).
